# KNOWN GAP — Core #8, BOTH BACKENDS WRONG (≥2 bugs), plus a Core #10 accept.
#
# A `Mutex[T]` reached through an EXPLICITLY-MUTABLE `&` borrow param loses
# writes made through its Guard, then dangles. MEASURED 2026-08-05, binaries
# invoked directly (never through a pipe):
#
#   C backend    : prints `40`  (the two increments VANISH) then SIGSEGV (139)
#   LLVM backend : no output at all,                              SIGSEGV (139)
#   gg check     : "OK: no semantic errors"  <-- ACCEPTED
#
# INTENDED: `42`, exit 0. `bump` takes a mutable borrow of the caller's Mutex,
# so both increments must be observable by the caller's own `.lock()`.
#
# ROOT CAUSE (measured, not hypothesised -- an earlier "binds a COPY" guess and a
# later "release is not emitted" guess were BOTH WRONG): a RECEIVER-ABI mismatch.
# `Mutex[int]` is itself a pointer -- `typedef GorgetMutex* Mutex__int64_t` -- so
# a `&` param lowers to `GorgetMutex**` while the runtime wants `GorgetMutex*` by
# value. The callee reinterprets the CALLER'S STACK FRAME as a GorgetMutex.
# Proof: patching one line of the emitted C (`__v0 = *(void**)__p0;`) gives 42,
# exit 0. The release IS emitted -- it releases a fake mutex.
#
# Lost writes, SEGV and deadlock are ONE mechanism reading different offsets of
# that fake struct, so WHICH face you see is stack-layout dependent. Trust the
# mechanism, not this file's recorded symptom, if they ever disagree.
#
# The real axis is `SelfConvention::ByValue` (12 protocols: Shared, Weak, Mutex,
# RWLock, Thread, AtomicInt, AtomicBool, Barrier, WaitGroup, Semaphore, OnceFlag,
# TaskGroup) -- NOT the `&` sigil, which is only one route to it. See TODO.md.
#
# SCOPE -- one cell of a wider class. Two statements, both true, do not confuse
# them:
#   WITHIN THIS SHAPE the sigil is what flips it, measured:
#     `Mutex[int] &m`  -> broken (this fixture)
#     `Mutex[int] m`   -> CORRECT; a bare param is already a borrow under CoW
#                         default-borrow, and mutex_alias.gg bumps 40 -> 42
#                         through two bare-param calls. So the EXPLICITLY
#                         mutable form is the broken one -- counter-intuitive,
#                         and worth keeping in the record.
#     inner type is NOT it: Mutex[String]+bare is correct, Mutex[int]+& breaks.
#   BUT THE CLASS AXIS IS NOT THE SIGIL. It is `SelfConvention::ByValue`. The
#   sigil is merely one way a pointer-typed receiver reaches a by-value callee;
#   a scout reports sigil-free routes (struct field, collection element) that
#   fail the same way. Fixing only the sigil would leave those live.
#
# COVERAGE HOLE THIS RECORDS: 43 fixtures use `Mutex[`; ZERO pass one by `&`.
# `mutex_alias.gg` was written as the Core #8 gate for this very family and
# samples only the bare-param cell -- a Core #12 axis-incompleteness in a
# fixture whose NAME ("alias") reads as if it covered borrows.
#
# Distinct from the filed Mutex drop-strategy family (TODO.md, Inc-A/B/C/D):
# that class is about WHO FREES a sync handle. This is about the borrow not
# being a borrow at all -- writes are lost even before anything is freed.
#
# Graduate out of known_gaps/ (un-ignore + move) the round this is fixed.

void bump(Mutex[int] &m):
    Guard[int] g = m.lock()
    int v = g.get()
    g.set(v + 1)

void main():
    Mutex[int] m = Mutex[int](40)
    bump(&m)
    bump(&m)
    Guard[int] g = m.lock()
    print(g.get())
